Create, build and load a PHP extension to see how its PHP stub and Zig implementation fit together.

# [Quick start]($heading.id('quick-start'))

## [Generating the extension]($heading.id('generate-extension'))

Run the following command to create an extension. The `phpz_skel.php` release asset is a standalone PHP script that generates an extension using phpz 0.1.0.

```sh
curl -fsSL https://github.com/happystraw/phpz/releases/download/0.1.0/phpz_skel.php | php -- --ext my_php_extension
```

On PHP 8.2, download the script and run it locally.

```sh
curl -fsSLO https://github.com/happystraw/phpz/releases/download/0.1.0/phpz_skel.php
php phpz_skel.php --ext my_php_extension
```

The generated project includes these files:

```
my_php_extension/
├── build.zig                    # Build configuration
├── build.zig.zon                # Project metadata and dependencies
├── my_php_extension.stub.php    # PHP API declarations
├── my_php_extension_arginfo.h   # Generated arginfo and registration code
├── my_php_extension.h           # Includes phpz and the arginfo header
├── src/
│   └── root.zig                 # Zig implementation and module registration
└── tests/
    ├── hello.phpt               # Test for hello()
    ├── greet.phpt               # Test for greet()
    └── counter.phpt             # Tests for Counter
```

## [Building and calling the extension]($heading.id('build-and-call'))

```sh
cd my_php_extension
zig build -Dphp-include-dir="$(php-config --include-dir)"

php -n -dextension=./modules/my_php_extension.so -r 'hello();'
php -n -dextension=./modules/my_php_extension.so -r 'echo greet("World"), PHP_EOL;'
php -n -dextension=./modules/my_php_extension.so -r '$c = new Counter(5); $c->add(3); echo $c->value(), PHP_EOL;'
```

The three commands print the following output, in order:

```
Hello from Zig!
Hello, World!
8
```

`-n` skips `php.ini`. `-dextension` loads the extension for this process.

On Windows, load `modules/php_my_php_extension.dll`. See [Building with MSVC on Windows]($link.page('development/windows').ref('windows-msvc')) for build options.

# [PHP API]($heading.id('php-api'))

`my_php_extension.stub.php` declares the functions, classes and types that PHP sees:

```php
<?php

/**
 * @generate-legacy-arginfo 80200
 * @generate-class-entries
 * @undocumentable
 */

function hello(): void {}
function greet(string $name): string {}

class Counter
{
    public function __construct(int $n = 0) {}
    public function add(int $n): void {}
    public function dec(int $n): void {}
    public function value(): int {}
}
```

The stub defines function names, parameter names and types, default values, and return types. For example, `greet(string $name): string` accepts and returns a string. With `Counter::__construct(int $n = 0)`, callers can omit the initial count and use the default of `0`.

As in a C extension built with PHP's own tooling, `gen_stub.php` reads the stub and writes arginfo, function tables and class registration code to `my_php_extension_arginfo.h`. The empty PHP function bodies never run. Edit the stub to change the API and let the generator maintain the header.

# [Zig implementation]($heading.id('zig-implementation'))

`src/root.zig` implements the API declared in the stub. Start with the module and its functions.

## [Registering the module]($heading.id('register-module'))

```zig
const std = @import("std");
const phpz = @import("phpz");

comptime {
    const extension = @import("extension_info");
    phpz.functions(functions, .{});
    phpz.module(.{
        .name = extension.name,
        .version = extension.version,
        .classes = &.{
            phpz.Class("Counter", Counter, .{}),
        },
    });
}
```

- `phpz.functions(functions, .{})` exports handlers for the public functions in `functions` and binds them to entries in the generated Zend function table.
- `phpz.Class("Counter", Counter, .{})` binds the PHP class `Counter` to the Zig struct `Counter`.
- `phpz.module()` exports the module entry and registers the listed classes at module startup. `build.zig` supplies the extension name and version.

## [Implement functions]($heading.id('implement-functions'))

```zig
const functions = struct {
    pub fn hello() void {
        _ = phpz.printf("Hello from Zig!\n", .{});
    }

    pub fn greet(name: []const u8) !*phpz.zend.String {
        var buffer: [256]u8 = undefined;
        const result = try std.fmt.bufPrint(&buffer, "Hello, {s}!", .{name});
        return .init(result, false);
    }
};
```

`hello()` takes no arguments and returns no value. It writes a greeting with `phpz.printf()`. For `greet()`, the wrapper reads the PHP argument and sets the PHP return value according to the Zig signature:

| PHP stub | Zig implementation |
| --- | --- |
| `greet` | `functions.greet` matches the declared name |
| `string $name` | `name: []const u8` borrows the string's bytes |
| `: string` | `*phpz.zend.String` gives PHP the newly allocated string |

`!*phpz.zend.String` returns a string on success or a Zig error on failure. `String.init` copies the bytes while the local buffer is valid. The PHP return value takes ownership of one reference. Returning a slice of the local buffer would leave the wrapper reading it after the handler returns. This example reports an error if the greeting exceeds 256 bytes.

## [A class example]($heading.id('implement-classes'))

The skeleton also includes `Counter`. The PHP example above creates an instance, changes its state and reads the result. See [Classes, properties and methods]($link.page('reference/registration/classes')) for constructors, class bindings and per-instance Zig data.

# [Testing and updating the extension]($heading.id('run-tests'))

`zig build run-tests` runs the project's PHPT tests using the copy of `run-tests.php` bundled with phpz:

```sh
zig build run-tests -Dphp-include-dir="$(php-config --include-dir)"
```

After editing `my_php_extension.stub.php`, regenerate the arginfo header, then build and test:

```sh
zig build gen-stub
zig build run-tests -Dphp-include-dir="$(php-config --include-dir)"
```

`zig build gen-stub` runs the bundled `gen_stub.php` and requires the PHP tokenizer extension. After a Zig-only change, run the build or tests directly.

